<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Singhasari</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Singhasari was a Javanese kingdom in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in the thirteenth century. It expanded its influence into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, challenging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and was overthrown in 1292. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> note that Majapahit arose from Singhasari's decline under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/singhasari.jpg" alt="Singhasari" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Singhasari_Kingdom_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Singhasari, also called Tumapel, was a Javanese Hindu-Buddhist kingdom in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> that succeeded the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> as the dominant power there. Its name comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> singha, &quot;lion&quot;, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> sari, which could mean either &quot;essence&quot; or &quot;to sleep&quot;. Most of what is told of its founding comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a>, which mixes in mythical elements. By that account <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ken-arok">Ken Arok</a>, an orphan, rose from servant of the regional ruler Tunggul Ametung at Tumapel to ruler of Java, killed Tunggul Ametung, and was himself later assassinated by Anusapati, Tunggul Ametung's son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ken-arok">Ken Arok</a> is considered the founder of the Rajasa dynasty, from which both the Singhasari and the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> rulers descended. Anusapati was killed by Ken Arok's son Panji Tohjaya, who reigned only a few months in 1248 before his nephews Ranga Wuni and Mahisha Champaka revolted and ruled together as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a> and Narasimhamurti.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a>, the fifth ruler, reigned from 1268. In 1275 he launched the Pamalayu naval expedition against the weakened remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and in 1284 he led an expedition that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> into the kingdom. He also sent troops and envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a>, Pahang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and the Maluku islands, and allied himself with the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> demanding tribute in 1280, 1281 and 1289, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a> refused each time, scarring the face of the envoy Meng Ki. In 1292 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a> of Daha, assisted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-wiraraja">Arya Wiraraja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, attacked from north and south while Kertanegara's forces were spread thin. His southern army sacked the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-aceh">Kutaraja</a> and killed the king during a Tantra ceremony, ending the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a>'s son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> escaped, took refuge under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-wiraraja">Arya Wiraraja</a>, and was allowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a> to open a settlement in the Tarik forest, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> after its bitter maja fruits. When a Mongol fleet of about 1,000 ships reached the coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuban">Tuban</a> in early 1293, Wijaya allied with it against Daha, then turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and forced them off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> on 31 May 1293. He took the throne as Kertajasa Jayawardhana on 12 November 1293, founding the Majapahit Empire, which took over Singhasari's territory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Singhasari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Majapahit Empire was established in the late 13th century in eastern Java after the collapse of the <b>Singhasari</b> kingdom, whose last king, Kertanegara, was killed by the rebel Jayakatwang of Kediri in 1292.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Strait of Malacca had been dominated by Srivijaya, centred on Palembang, until the Cholas weakened it in the 11th century, and by the end of the 13th by the Javanese <b>Singhasari</b> and Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rise of the Javanese kingdoms of Kediri, then <b>Singhasari</b> and Majapahit, created a powerful rival centre that contested Srivijayan cultural and commercial influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1292, while <b>Singhasari's</b> army was away in Sumatra, he attacked, killed Kertanegara and restored the old royal seat at Daha.</blockquote><a href="/state/gelang-gelang">Gelang-gelang</a></li><li><blockquote>Ken Arok defeated him at Ganter in 1222 and founded <b>Singhasari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kediri-kingdom">Kediri Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
